<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wang Jingwei</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1883–1944</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Wang Zhaoming</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wang Jingwei was a leading figure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Chinese Nationalist Party</a>, a close associate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a> and a rival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a>. In 1938 he broke with Chiang and sought peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, and in 1940 he became head of a Japanese-sponsored Reorganised National Government at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> that absorbed the earlier collaborationist regimes. He died in Japan in 1944.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wang-jingwei.jpg" alt="Wang Jingwei" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%B1%AA%E7%B2%BE%E8%A1%9B%E7%85%A7%E7%89%87.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
